  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Intermediate Results Indicator</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="1" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">6.  Share of permit applications assessed for compliance with Building Code by the National Building Code Assessment Unit (BCAU) (percent of total).</narrative>
    </title>
    <baseline iso-date="2022-12-15" value="Nil (2022)" year="2022">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN"></narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-12-15"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="1900-01-01"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">[Slow Progress] The new Building Code Assessment Unit (BCAU) is only partially operational starting 2024. The Government is committed to the reform but is constrained by capacity available in-country and in the interim it is benefiting from DFAT TA to progress implementation of the Building Code. The BCAU is using the Building Code to assess its pipeline of public buildings before commencing phase 2 of the reform for private dwellings. As the public building pilot has just initiated (i.e., a permit is underway for the Deputy Prime Minister&#xe2;&#x20ac;&#x2122;s house), there is no update to the value for 2023. The target is likely to be achieved by 2027 but delays could be expected particularly with assessing private dwellings. There is a need to build and sustain the capacity of BCAU and conduct island-wide consultations on the phase 2 rollout to ensure successful and sustainable reform implementation.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual value="0"></actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
